othing could prepare you for like  actually happening and  seeing  someone you love  like  just  in a hospital bed um  and uh  yeah it was  it was  really traumatic for all of us  do you feel there was anything you could  have done  no  i think that if he  if he hadn't died that day if he hadn't  overdosed  when he did it would have happened at  some point  i know that i i don't i don't know that  he ever would have  gotten sober and stayed sober  unfortunately  um  i also think a part of him like really  liked that lifestyle of  living on the streets and  [Music]  i don't know i  i mean  he  i think he felt  in a way like most  at home  there  like more at home than he did at his  actual home with us on the streets  yeah  yeah i've seen that a lot yeah  he had a it was like his family and  yeah they just don't feel comfortable  with a better situation a better living  situation  exactly  and  yeah i i think i mean i know he he was  also he struggled with depression and  there was a you know mental illness too  yeah  he was  i know he was suffering a lot  i don't think he really  cared  whether he lived or died like i don't  think he would have ever like  tried to kill himself but  i  i just don't think he really cared  towards the end  either way  do you feel like this is just the way he  was  he was just born this way genetically it  was inside him from birth i do  and i think  i think it was  mostly  genetic because again we were raised the  same way with the same loving parents  but  i think  him being adopted played a huge part in  it  he had that  feeling of  you know not being wanted or abandoned  from very early on and i don't think he  ever really like  came to terms with that i guess and i  think he  just never felt like he fit in and he  felt  very lonely  and sad  a lot of the time  do you feel like there's anything you've  learned from all of this  i think i've learned a lot  i've learned i i think  when i was going through this with him  um i had a lot of anger i still do i  still  um the therapy has helped a lot but  i was very angry at him  and  and i  and i don't think i really understood  addiction the way i do now  and  now  i've  i feel like i  have so much more  love and compassion for  people who are suffering with addiction  and i've learned that  people who use drugs people who are  addicted to drugs  or  put a needle in their arm whatever  whatever it is  deserve just as much love and compassion  as  anyone else  because i see my brother and  all addicts now  um  you feel like it's a mental  illness more than a  predisposition to drug addiction  uh  to me i see the mindset  that they have yeah  opens the door for drug use yeah which  always leads to an addiction right  yeah i think it maybe it's a  mix of both yeah yeah  i've also learned that  um i feel like  uh i treat my relationships now very  differently than i did  in the past before losing my brother i  really um  treasure  uh all of my relationships with you know  especially with my parents and my  friends and i just  i mean  life is  very precious  and it could end  at any time  so i just i feel like i have  a lot more love  now in me  that's great  liz thank you so much for sharing your  story thank you sorry about your brother  thank you  thank you  you
 

